      "better_line": "Attack with Icetill Explorer for 2 free damage against an empty board with no untapped mana.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 45,
      "player": "Gem3F Coach",
      "decision_index": 53,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Gem3F Coach has 3 mana available (Eldrazi Temple + Ghost Quarter) and could cast Kozilek's Command for X=1, choosing 'scry 1, draw a card' plus another mode (like creating a spawn token or exiling graveyard cards to weaken potential Nethergoyf). This is essentially a free cantrip with upside that wastes no resources since the mana would otherwise go unused.",
      "action_taken": "Passed without casting Kozilek's Command, leaving mana unused.",
      "better_line": "Cast Kozilek's Command for X=1 using Eldrazi Temple and Ghost Quarter, choosing 'scry 1 and draw a card' plus 'create 1 Eldrazi Spawn token' or 'exile 1 card from opponent's graveyard' to gain card advantage and board presence.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 56,
      "player": "Gem3F Coach",
      "decision_index": 68,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "The player's reasoning incorrectly states they already have a Pithing Needle in play - they don't. With only 1 land on the battlefield and Ghost Quarter threatening their mana development, Pithing Needle naming Ghost Quarter would protect their fragile mana base. Shadowspear has no immediate impact since there are no creatures on board to equip.",
      "action_taken": "Chose to cast Shadowspear, an equipment with no creatures to equip",
      "better_line": "Cast Pithing Needle naming Ghost Quarter to protect the thin mana base from further land destruction, preserving the ability to develop lands and eventually cast meaningful spells",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 58,
      "player": "Qwen3L Sarge",
      "decision_index": 71,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Coach declined to activate Ghost Quarter to destroy the opponent's only land (Eldrazi Temple) while having a massive mana advantage and Icetill Explorer to replay Ghost Quarter from graveyard. Destroying the opponent's sole land would further lock them out of the game.",
      "action_taken": "Passed priority without taking any action in precombat main phase.",
      "better_line": "Activate Ghost Quarter targeting opponent's Eldrazi Temple to destroy their only land, severely limiting their ability to recover. Icetill Explorer allows replaying Ghost Quarter from graveyard later.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 64,
      "player": "Gem3F Coach",
      "decision_index": 82,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Gem3F Coach passed on using Ghost Quarter to destroy Qwen3L Sarge's only land (Eldrazi Temple), which would have been devastating against an already mana-screwed opponent. While Sarge could search for a basic land, losing Eldrazi Temple's special mana production while already behind would be crippling. Additionally, Kozilek's Command could have been cast for value (scry + draw) with available mana.",
      "action_taken": "Passed without using any instants or activated abilities during combat.",
      "better_line": "Activate Ghost Quarter targeting Eldrazi Temple to destroy the opponent's only land, further punishing their mana screw. Could also consider casting Kozilek's Command for card advantage.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 71,
      "player": "Gem3F Coach",
      "decision_index": 92,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "When ordering cards to put on top of library (from Kozilek's Command scry or similar effect), Gem3F Coach put Eldrazi Temple on top instead of Devourer of Destiny. With Emrakul, the Promised End already in hand and the board in a mid-game state, Devourer of Destiny would be the stronger card to have on top - it's a 6/6 that exiles a colored permanent on cast, providing both a threat and removal. Eldrazi Temple is a land that adds colorless mana, but the player already has significant mana production (2 Ugin's Labyrinths, 2 Talismans, Ghost Quarter, Eldrazi Temple, and Icetill Explorer). Devourer of Destiny would help cast Emrakul faster (creature card type in graveyard if it dies, or just as a strong play before Emrakul), and its exile trigger is very relevant against the opponent's board. The extra mana from Eldrazi Temple is less impactful when you already have 7+ mana sources.",
      "action_taken": "Put Eldrazi Temple on top of library instead of Devourer of Destiny",
      "better_line": "Put Devourer of Destiny on top of library to have access to a powerful threat with an exile trigger, which would also help advance the gameplan toward casting Emrakul",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 76,
      "player": "Gem3F Coach",
      "decision_index": 108,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Gem3F Coach declined to return World Breaker from graveyard to hand using its activated ability ({2}{C}, sacrifice a land). With Ghost Quarter available as the sacrificed land (relatively low value) and sufficient mana, this would have recovered a powerful 7-mana threat that also triggers Kozilek's Return from graveyard when cast, and exiles a permanent on cast.",
      "action_taken": "Declined to activate World Breaker's return ability, letting the Devourer of Destiny trigger resolve without interaction.",
      "better_line": "Activate World Breaker's ability by paying {2}{C} and sacrificing Ghost Quarter to return World Breaker to hand, providing a future powerful threat and potential Kozilek's Return trigger.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 86,
      "player": "Gem3F Coach",
      "decision_index": 118,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Attempting to cast Kozilek's Command before playing a land this turn. With only 3 lands on board (Eldrazi Temple, Urborg, and one other), playing an Eldrazi Temple from hand first would add {C}{C} for Eldrazi spells, significantly increasing the X value of Kozilek's Command. Current mana allows roughly X=2, but playing Eldrazi Temple first could enable X=4.",
      "action_taken": "Chose to cast Kozilek's Command before making a land drop",
      "better_line": "Play Eldrazi Temple from hand first, then cast Kozilek's Command with a higher X value to get more value from the chosen modes (more tokens, bigger scry, exile a bigger creature, or exile more graveyard cards)",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 98,
      "player": "Qwen3L Sarge",
      "decision_index": 136,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Casting Kozilek's Command with X=0 provides minimal value (at best drawing one card for 2 mana) while ignoring Pithing Needle, which could shut down Ghost Quarter (protecting their lands at 7 life) or Ugin's Labyrinth (disrupting opponent's mana). Additionally, casting a spell before playing a land drop is a sequencing error - Eldrazi Temple should be played first to maximize available mana.",
      "action_taken": "Chose to cast Kozilek's Command with X=0 before playing a land",
      "better_line": "Play Eldrazi Temple first, then cast Pithing Needle naming Ghost Quarter (to protect lands) or Ugin's Labyrinth (to disrupt opponent's mana engine), saving Kozilek's Command for a turn where X can be meaningful",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 100,
      "player": "Qwen3L Sarge",
      "decision_index": 138,
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Kozilek's Command is on the stack and requires selecting a target player for the 'create tokens' mode. The player chose 'False' to try to cancel, but this is a misunderstanding of how the game works - you cannot cancel a spell that's already on the stack and resolving. The correct choice is to select themselves (Qwen3L Sarge) to receive the Eldrazi Spawn tokens, which would give them 0/1 tokens that can be sacrificed for colorless mana. Instead, by choosing False/canceling incorrectly, the tokens likely default to the opponent or the choice is wasted. Creating tokens for yourself is pure upside - free mana sources and chump blockers when you're at 7 life.",
      "action_taken": "Chose False to try to cancel the token creation mode of Kozilek's Command, misunderstanding the game state.",
      "better_line": "Choose Qwen3L Sarge (yourself) as the target player to receive the Eldrazi Spawn tokens, gaining free blockers and mana sources while at a dangerously low 7 life.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 102,
      "player": "Qwen3L Sarge",
      "decision_index": 140,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "When resolving Kozilek's Command and selecting a player for the 'create tokens' mode, the player chose Gem3F Coach, giving the opponent Eldrazi Spawn tokens. Since Kozilek's Command says 'Target player creates X 0/1 colorless Eldrazi Spawn creature tokens,' choosing yourself would give you the tokens (which can be sacrificed for mana), while choosing the opponent gives them free blockers and mana sources. The player's own reasoning acknowledges this is giving the opponent tokens but proceeds anyway out of apparent confusion with the interface.",
      "action_taken": "Chose Gem3F Coach as the player to receive Eldrazi Spawn tokens from Kozilek's Command.",
      "better_line": "Choose Qwen3L Sarge (self) as the target player for the create tokens mode, gaining the Eldrazi Spawn tokens for your own use as blockers and mana sources.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 104,
      "player": "Qwen3L Sarge",
      "decision_index": 141,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Kozilek's Command's second mode (scry X then draw a card) was being targeted. The caster (Qwen3L Sarge) chose 'False' which defaulted to letting the opponent (Gem3F Coach) scry and draw a card instead of targeting themselves. This is a pure-upside card draw/scry ability that should always target yourself when you're the one casting the spell. Giving the opponent card advantage for free is a significant mistake, especially when at 7 life facing a board with Sowing Mycospawn.",
      "action_taken": "Selected Gem3F Coach as the player who scries and draws, giving the opponent free card advantage.",
      "better_line": "Select Qwen3L Sarge (yourself) as the target for scry X and draw a card, gaining the card advantage from your own spell.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 105,
      "player": "Qwen3L Sarge",
      "decision_index": 143,
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Chose to give beneficial Eldrazi Spawn tokens to the opponent instead of themselves. At 7 life with minimal board presence, Qwen3L Sarge desperately needs chump blockers and mana acceleration. The 0/1 Spawn tokens would serve both purposes, while giving them to the opponent only strengthens their already dominant position.",
      "action_taken": "Selected Gem3F Coach as the player to receive Eldrazi Spawn tokens from Kozilek's Command",
      "better_line": "Select Qwen3L Sarge (themselves) to receive the Eldrazi Spawn tokens for chump blocking and mana generation",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 109,
      "player": "Qwen3L Sarge",
      "decision_index": 148,
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Kozilek's Command was on the stack with a mode asking Qwen3L Sarge to select a player for 'scries then draw'. This is a pure upside ability - selecting yourself lets you scry and draw a card for free. Instead, the player chose 'False' (declined/cancelled), which caused them to lose the game. The player's reasoning indicates frustration with the interface rather than any strategic consideration.",
      "action_taken": "Chose 'False' (declined the selection), which resulted in losing the game.",
      "better_line": "Select 'Qwen3L Sarge' (yourself) to scry and draw a card, gaining card advantage. Even if the Command was cast by the opponent, getting to scry and draw is beneficial. The player should have engaged with the prompt rather than refusing it out of frustration.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 109,
      "player": "Qwen3L Sarge",
      "decision_index": 148,
    }
